s who were doing  something similar to us and I think  that's not true and I and I one of the  things I wanted to do in the book I  tried to do is to show the ways that  even when you look at how meditation and  his fruits are being discussed by the  most intellectual strata of Buddhists  they connect it to all the domains so  they they were interested in in  attaining success in meditation to be  able to predict the future to be able to  communicate with the gods even if they  thought there their techniques were  superior to those say that would be sort  of practiced by fortune tellers on the  streets of you know the capital of  people China it was the same kind of  activity and they were still hoping for  the same kinds of say visions or dreams  for confirming their success that you  would find in many different parts of  both Buddhism but also other forms of  did you look at India and what was going  on and how they were practicing Buddhism  there during the same period that they  were in China and are there similarities  dissimilarities talk about that a little  bit yes that's a very large question of  course one of the one of the arguments I  do make in the book is that the  collection of these medieval Chinese  sources for thinking about Buddhist  meditation they're important from the  perspective of China but they're also  important from the perspective of kind  of a broader trans Asian history of  Buddhism so the the material that  survives for the study of Indian  Buddhism we have sort of texts and  sermons attributed to the Buddha himself  and then we have a lot much later of  material there is actually this large  kind of maybe 400 year window in the  middle where there aren't a lot of  sources to study Indian Buddhism so the  the meditation texts that I examine in  my book  many of them were translated from Indian  languages Sanskrit really kind of  medieval other forms of Indian languages  at this time and some were composed in  China so they I think there is their  differences and their particularity is  to the Chinese situation but there's  also a way in which China because of the  tendency of texts to get preserved  they're better than they do in India  where it's actually there's material  reasons for this too because it's very  hot and wet texts are manuscripts from  the medieval times are much rarer in  China you have you have many more this  actually gives us a window onto Indian  Buddhism as well and many of the things  that I were just discussing I think this  this is true of Indian Buddhist  meditation at this time as well so one  of the things I did want to sort of  argue in the book is that this is not a  kind of corruption of Buddhism which is  often how these things get presented  that you have a sort of early pure  tradition of rational meditation not  influenced by all this fortune-telling  stuff and then later you know ask people  stop understanding it or to appeal to  the masses of people or something like  that these things so I do want to argue  and I  do argue in the book that that's that's  not really a correct understanding this  is and through this material in China  from the fourth and fifth century we can  really see that across China and in  Central Asia and then in India as well  even if the view into India is a little  bit murkier because of the long distance  and the relatively fewer direct sources  so ultimately what do you conclude in  your book and what would you like  readers to take away from it yeah well I  I think the main thing that I want  readers to take away from it is I mean  on the one hand there is a specific  question about the history of annotation  in in China in East Asia we have the  forms or this tradition really a sub  sect of East Asian Buddhism Zen it's  quite well known that does have an  approach which maybe looks a little more  familiar to us  emphasizing meditation that's a very  sort of rational abstract procedure so  on the one hand you know specifically t