ing else but hopefully  something in my book will do that as  well okay fascinating so what are next  steps for you well now so I this  Buddhist meditation topic I've been  working on for a long time and now I'm  starting a new project associated with  the studying the history of translation  in Indian Chinese Buddhism so as I said  before the process of the transmission  of Buddhism from India to China was  accompanied among other things by the  translation of a large number of Indian  Buddhist scriptures this really from a  world historical point of view is  probably almost certainly one of the  largest if not the largest sustained  translation projects in the pre-modern  world the volume of material translated  the distance that had to be negotiated  in terms of the difference between  Indian languages and Chinese the  conceptual differences and this went on  for many hundreds of years and it forms  the basis of the Buddhist Canon in East  Asia but but the process of this  translation the strategies that were  adopted by translators the difficulties  they encountered so I'm doing a project  now associated with the translation that  took place really in the first two or  three hundred years of this process  where the language is often quite  different from what we find in later  literature in China it's quite harder to  understand but you can really see the  the early Chinese interpreters of Indian  Buddhism struggling to make sense of  what at the time was a very very new and  different religion but also a sort of  philosophy and this this will be this is  a something I've long been interested in  and of course it's connected to any  aspect of studying Buddhism in East Asia  but now to focus specifically on this  problem of translation how that took  place and the issues that all right well  we will look forward to hearing more  from you then thank you yes thank you  for being here and sharing your work  with us for more information about  Professor Green and his